{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"172e PARTIALLY IMPLEMENTED — one real defect in MY implementation, unresolved. Do not treat as done.\n\nDONE and verified:\n- model_classes.resolve_model_string(value) — the shared reserved-word resolver both 172e and 7761\n  consume. TRIVIAL_CLASS/STANDARD_CLASS/FRONTIER_CLASS constants so call sites never spell bare\n  strings. 23/23 tests; mutation-checked (restricting the reserved set to \"standard\" alone lets\n  \"trivial\" through as a literal model id, and the test catches it).\n- _verifier_cfg (plan_review/__init__.py) now resolves the STANDARD class regardless of cfg.model.\n- completion.py's PARALLEL copy of the same equality test replaced by _verifier_model_for_completion().\n- New tests/unit/test_verifier_class.py: 8 tests, RED-first. The RED output was exactly the defect\n  matrix — anthropic:claude-opus-4-8 left the verifier on opus, openai:gpt-4o left it on gpt-4o.\n- ruff + mypy clean. 31 passed across the new + model_classes suites.\n\nTHE DEFECT I INTRODUCED, found by an EXISTING test (test_verifier_cfg_downgrades_to_sonnet_by_default):\n\n  expected \"claude-sonnet-4-6\"   (bare)\n  got      \"anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6\"  (provider-qualified)\n\nresolve_class ALWAYS emits a provider-qualified id, so with NO class configured the verifier model\nstring changes form even though it names the same model. My plan promised this change would be\n\"byte-identical to today for every existing deployment\". It is NOT. Functionally equivalent (a bare\nclaude-* id infers provider anthropic), but the string differs, so it will show up in usage logs,\nprovider_provenance, and any test or consumer comparing the literal.\n\nTWO OPTIONS, deliberately NOT decided here:\n  (a) make the promise true — return the slot's model UNQUALIFIED when no class is configured and\n      the slot is at its built-in default. Preserves byte-identity; costs a special case.\n  (b) accept the qualified form and CORRECT the plan's byte-identical claim, updating the existing\n      test. Arguably better long-term (a qualified id is unambiguous and is what provenance wants),\n      but it is a real behaviour change that must be stated, not slipped in.\nRecommend (b) with the claim corrected, but this is a plan-level decision — it changes what 172e\npromises, so it should be re-reviewed rather than chosen by the implementer.\n\nSEPARATELY, a legitimate contract change still pending:\ntest_verifier_cfg_honors_explicit_operator_override pins the OLD rule (\"an operator who explicitly\nchose a non-default model keeps it\"). That rule IS the defect 172e removes — under classes the\nverifier always resolves `standard` and the operator steers it via the class config. That test must\nbe updated to the new contract WITH justification in the commit message. It is not a test to\n\"fix away\" quietly; it encodes the behaviour this ticket intentionally changes.\n\nNOT STARTED: 7761 (ladder rungs -> classes; consumes the same resolve_model_string), d23e (blocked).\ncc33 was dispatched to /Users/joeoakhart/rebar-wt-cc33 and was still running at handoff.\ncb6f is COMPLETE and verified in /Users/joeoakhart/rebar-wt-cb6f (14 tests, doc gate clean,\nread_reserved_section untouched, deep-merge mutation-checked) but is NOT committed or pushed."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785508409703880001,"uuid":"334b0341-7e5f-417e-ac12-652d80ae6052"}